and sometimes the sacred vessels and other treasures of the church, are kept. {{smaller|Such an apartment is also called ''sacristy'' or ''vestry-room''. It is now, in Anglican churches, generally under the same roof with the church, and is usually placed at one side of the chancel.}}
{{smaller block|A ''vestry'' or sanctuary, on the Gospel side of the altar.
{{right|''J. Fergusson'', Hist. Arch., I. 426.}}}}

'''2.''' In ''non-liturgical churches'', a room or building attached to a church, and used for the Sabbath-school, the weekly prayer-meetings, religious services, etc.; a chapel.—'''3.''' In ''Eng. eccles. law'', and in ''Amer. colonial law'': (''a'') A meeting of the inhabitants or ratepayers of a parish for the despatch of the official business of the parish. (''b'') A meeting or a board consisting of representatives of the ratepayers at large, all of whom are entitled to vote in their election. {{smaller|It is not essential to the validity of the meeting that it be held in the vestry, or even in connection with the church-building. The general charge of the church property is intrusted to the vestry, together with certain administrative duties respecting the parish, such as the care of the poor, and sometimes the paving and lighting of the streets, etc.}}
{{smaller block|The farmers whom he met at ''vestry''.
{{right|''T. Hughes'', Tom Brown at Rugby, i. 3.}}}}

'''4.''' In the ''Prot. Epis. Ch.'' in the United States of America, a committee (chosen annually by the members of the congregation) who, in conjunction with the churchwardens, manage its temporal affairs. {{smaller|The time and manner of electing the vestrymen, and their rights and duties, are different in different dioceses, being determined by diocesan regulations. The vestry has a general charge of the temporalities of the church, and, in the case of a vacancy in the pastorate, is the official representative of the parish; but it exercises no ecclesiastical control over the rector, either}}

readings.c1s3:
{{smaller block|{{right|''Chaucer'', Good Women, l. 2691.}}}}
{{smaller block|As a ''vesture'' shalt thou fold them up, and they shall be changed.
{{right|Heb. i. 12.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Madam, with your pardon,
I kiss your ''vesture''.</poem>
{{right|''B. Jonson'', Alchemist, iv. 1.}}}}
'''2.''' That which invests or covers; covering generally; envelop; integument.
{{smaller block|The napless ''vesture'' of humility.
{{right|''Shak.'', Cor., ii. 1. 250.}}}}
'''3.''' In ''old law'': (''a'') All, except trees, that grows on or forms the covering of land: as, the ''vesture'' of an acre.
{{smaller block|The profits and advantages of the ''vesture'' and herbage of the garden called the Halgarth.
{{right|Quoted in ''Child’s Ballads'', V. 126.}}}}
{{smaller block|But the best ground is knowne by the ''vesture'' it beareth, as by the greatnesse of trees, or abundance of weeds.
{{right|''Capt. John Smith'', Works, I. 115.}}}}
(''b'') Investiture; seizin; possession. ='''Syn. 1''' and '''2.''' {{smaller|See ''raiment''.}}

'''vesture''' (ves′tṳ̄r), ''v. t.''; pret. and pp. ''vestured'', ppr. ''vesturing''. [< ''vesture'', ''n.''] To put vesture or clothing on; clothe; robe; vest.
{{smaller block|Wyllynge furthermore that he shuld bee honourably receaued and ''vestured'' with silke.
{{right|''R. Eden'', tr. of Paolo Giovio (First Books on America,}}
{{right|[ed. Arber, p. 309).}}}}
{{smaller block|We never tired of the graceful women walking through the streets ''vestured'' in garments of barbaric tint.
{{right|''Lathrop'', Spanish Vistas, p. 67.}}}}

